Here is a complete guide and working code for displaying **Vietnamese UTF-8 text** on a **GC9A01 (240x240 round LCD)** using **Arduino_GFX** on an **ESP32**.

---

### Key Requirements

1. **`Arduino_GFX_Library`** by Moon On Our Nation (for driving the GC9A01).
2. **`U8g2_for_Adafruit_GFX`** by olikraus (to render full UTF-8 Vietnamese glyphs on top of Adafruit/Arduino_GFX canvas).

---

### 1. Hardware Connections (ESP32 Standard SPI)

| GC9A01 Pin | ESP32 Pin | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **VCC** | 3.3V / 5V | Power Supply |
| **GND** | GND | Ground |
| **SCL / CLK** | GPIO 18 | SPI Clock (SCK) |
| **SDA / DIN** | GPIO 23 | SPI MOSI |
| **CS** | GPIO 5 | Chip Select |
| **DC** | GPIO 2 | Data / Command |
| **RST / RES** | GPIO 4 | Reset |
| **BLK / LED** | 3.3V (or GPIO 22 for PWM) | Backlight |

---

### 2. Required Libraries in Arduino IDE

Install the following via **Tools → Manage Libraries...**:
1. `GFX Library for Arduino` (by Moon On Our Nation)
2. `U8g2_for_Adafruit_GFX` (by olikraus)

---

### 3. Complete Working Code

```cpp
#include <Arduino_GFX_Library.h>
#include <U8g2_for_Adafruit_GFX.h>

// Pin Definitions for ESP32
#define TFT_SCK  18
#define TFT_MOSI 23
#define TFT_MISO GFX_NOT_DEFINED
#define TFT_CS   5
#define TFT_DC   2
#define TFT_RST  4
#define TFT_BL   22

// Initialize Arduino_GFX Bus and Display Driver
Arduino_DataBus *bus = new Arduino_ESP32SPI(TFT_DC, TFT_CS, TFT_SCK, TFT_MOSI, TFT_MISO);
Arduino_GFX *gfx = new Arduino_GC9A01(bus, TFT_RST, 0 /* rotation */, true /* IPS */);

// Bridge for UTF-8 U8g2 Fonts
U8G2_FOR_ADAFRUIT_GFX u8g2Fonts;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Backlight setup
  pinMode(TFT_BL, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(TFT_BL, HIGH);

  // Initialize GC9A01 Display
  if (!gfx->begin()) {
    Serial.println("gfx->begin() failed!");
  }
  gfx->fillScreen(BLACK);

  // Connect U8g2 to Arduino_GFX
  u8g2Fonts.begin(*gfx);
  u8g2Fonts.setFontMode(1);                   // Transparent background mode
  u8g2Fonts.setFontDirection(0);              // Left-to-right

  // Draw decorative outer circle (for round GC9A01)
  gfx->drawCircle(120, 120, 118, BLUE);
  gfx->drawCircle(120, 120, 115, CYAN);

  // Print Header
  u8g2Fonts.setFont(u8g2_font_helvB12_tf);
  u8g2Fonts.setForegroundColor(YELLOW);
  u8g2Fonts.setCursor(55, 60);
  u8g2Fonts.print("ESP32 & GC9A01");

  // Vietnamese UTF-8 Fonts available in U8g2:
  // - u8g2_font_unifont_t_vietnamese1
  // - u8g2_font_unifont_t_vietnamese2
  // - u8g2_font_cu12_t_vietnamese1
  u8g2Fonts.setFont(u8g2_font_unifont_t_vietnamese1);

  // Line 1: Basic Vietnamese with full accents
  u8g2Fonts.setForegroundColor(WHITE);
  u8g2Fonts.setCursor(45, 105);
  u8g2Fonts.print("Xin chào Việt Nam!");

  // Line 2: Diacritics demonstration
  u8g2Fonts.setForegroundColor(GREEN);
  u8g2Fonts.setCursor(35, 135);
  u8g2Fonts.print("Cà phê sữa đá");

  // Line 3: Complex vowels (ơ, ư, ă, â, đ)
  u8g2Fonts.setForegroundColor(ORANGE);
  u8g2Fonts.setCursor(40, 165);
  u8g2Fonts.print("Đồng Nai - Phở bò");

  // Line 4: Small status line
  u8g2Fonts.setFont(u8g2_font_6x12_te);
  u8g2Fonts.setForegroundColor(MAGENTA);
  u8g2Fonts.setCursor(65, 195);
  u8g2Fonts.print("UTF-8 OK (100%)");
}

void loop() {
  // Add animation or sensor updates here
  delay(1000);
}
```

---

### 4. Available Vietnamese Fonts in U8g2

| Font Name | Size / Style | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `u8g2_font_unifont_t_vietnamese1` | 16px Fixed | Standard full Vietnamese character set |
| `u8g2_font_unifont_t_vietnamese2` | 16px Fixed | Alternate set with extended tone glyphs |
| `u8g2_font_cu12_t_vietnamese1` | 12px Compact | Good for small status bars / data fields |
| `u8g2_font_t0_11_t_all` | 11px Small | Compact monospace with broad UTF-8 support |

---

### 5. Troubleshooting & Key Tips

1. **Source Code Encoding**: Ensure your `.ino` or `.cpp` file is saved with **UTF-8 encoding without BOM** so strings like `"Tiếng Việt"` compile properly.
2. **GC9A01 Round Clipping**: The display is circular (240x240), so coordinates near `(0, 0)` or `(240, 240)` will be hidden outside the bezel. Keep your text centered horizontally around `X = 30` to `X = 210`.
3. **Using S3 / C3 Variants**: If using ESP32-S3 or ESP32-C3, pass custom pins into `Arduino_ESP32SPI(TFT_DC, TFT_CS, TFT_SCK, TFT_MOSI, TFT_MISO)` matching your board's pinout.


